{#-
  An XCCDF tailoring file: the profile this host is actually judged against.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. A benchmark rule tests a MEANS, and occasionally the deployment reaches the same
  END by another one. Leaving such a rule selected produces a permanently failing check, and a
  permanently failing check is read exactly once -- after that it is scenery, and the next genuine
  failure beside it is scenery too. Deselecting it without writing down why is the opposite problem:
  the score looks better and nobody can reconstruct the argument.

  So every exclusion carries its reason into the profile DESCRIPTION.

  Not into an XML comment, and that is not a style choice: XML forbids `--` inside a comment, and
  these reasons are prose written by people who use dashes. oscap rejected the whole file with
  "Double hyphen within comment" on the first attempt (2026-09-20) -- the same trap this repository
  already records for the OWASP suppressions file, in a different XML dialect.

  Where it ends up, measured rather than assumed (2026-09-20) -- the first version of this comment
  claimed the HTML report carries it, and it does not:

      the ARF (arf-<date>.xml)   carries the reason verbatim. That is the archived, machine-readable
                                 result, so the argument travels with the evidence.
      the HTML report            does NOT carry it, but does name the tailored profile id, which is
                                 the thread back to this file.

  So the description is the right place for a different reason than the one first written down: not
  because a reader meets it in the report, but because it cannot be separated from the results.

  The profile EXTENDS the upstream one rather than restating it, so an scap-security-guide upgrade
  brings every new and changed rule with it -- only the named exclusions stay excluded.
-#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xccdf:Tailoring xmlns:xccdf="http://checklists.nist.gov/xccdf/1.2"
                 id="xccdf_{{ basetool_host_hardening_tailoring_ns }}_tailoring_basetool">
  <xccdf:benchmark href="{{ basetool_host_hardening_datastream }}"/>
  {#-
    A STABLE time, from basetool_host_hardening_tailoring_time, not `ansible_date_time`.

    The attribute is required -- oscap rejects the file without it ("The attribute 'time' is
    required but missing") -- but rendering "now" into it rewrote this file on every play, which is
    churn in the one file whose purpose is to record a decision that does not change. Measured
    2026-09-20, both ways.

    It also happens to be what the attribute means: when this VERSION of the tailoring was made,
    which is when somebody changed an exclusion, not when a play ran.
  -#}
  <xccdf:version time="{{ basetool_host_hardening_tailoring_time }}">1</xccdf:version>

  <xccdf:Profile id="{{ basetool_host_hardening_tailored_profile }}"
                 extends="{{ basetool_host_hardening_profile }}">
    <xccdf:title xml:lang="en">{{ basetool_host_hardening_profile | regex_replace('^.*_profile_', '') | e }}, as this deployment is judged</xccdf:title>
    <xccdf:description xml:lang="en">Managed by the basetool_host Ansible role; do not edit by hand.
Extends the upstream profile, so an scap-security-guide upgrade brings every new and changed rule
with it. Each deselection below is a decision with a reason, not a convenience.
{% for e in basetool_host_hardening_exclusions %}

DESELECTED: {{ e.rule | e }}
{{ e.reason | trim | e }}
{% endfor %}
</xccdf:description>
{% for e in basetool_host_hardening_exclusions %}
    <xccdf:select idref="xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_rule_{{ e.rule }}" selected="false"/>
{% endfor %}
  </xccdf:Profile>
</xccdf:Tailoring>
